WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.303
The Group Policy sunset runs as
a program, one setting at a time,

2
00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:07.388
in seven steps from
inventory to retirement.

3
00:00:07.388 --> 00:00:11.224
Every setting ends one of three
ways: migrate it to Intune,

4
00:00:11.224 --> 00:00:14.651
replace it with a cloud-native
pattern, or retire it.

5
00:00:14.651 --> 00:00:18.700
The coexistence rules matter most,
and the old guides had them wrong.

6
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.825
While a device is under both,
Group Policy wins by default.

7
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:27.250
There's a setting that flips that, but it
only covers part of the policy surface.

8
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.258
Defender settings and
Windows Hello for Business

9
00:00:30.258 --> 00:00:33.338
have to be removed from
Group Policy, not overridden.

10
00:00:33.338 --> 00:00:38.550
Group Policy preferences can leave
settings behind after a GPO is unlinked,

11
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.949
so each one needs a planned cleanup.

12
00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:45.950
And populations are unlinked one at a
time, because that's scoped and reversible.

13
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.375
Finally, measure, don't assume.

14
00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:52.263
The program runs Group Policy
analytics on its own exports.

15
00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:54.310
Then the fleet itself migrates.
